Which capability to build

Short answer: build a Failure Intelligence capability. The unit of analysis is the failure pattern, and the headline question it answers is: “Is the bank learning from its failures, or paying for the same ones repeatedly?”

The reasoning is that the 139,744 incidents are the only dataset that records what actually happened. The controls (6,666) are hypotheses about how failure is prevented. Issues and actions are the bank’s stated response. Incidents let you test whether those hypotheses and responses were right, which no dashboard or audit sample does at scale.

Part 1: The highest-value problem

Problem statement: The bank cannot demonstrate that remediation reduces recurrence of the failures that matter. It records failures, fixes them one at a time, and closes them, but it has no evidence-based view of which failure mechanisms keep returning, which fixes worked, and where its control framework does not touch the way it actually fails.

Why it matters

Recurrence is the most expensive form of failure: the same cause, paid for again, after management said it was fixed.
Regulators increasingly ask for evidence that remediation is effective, not just complete.
Capital, headcount and control investment are currently allocated against risk assessments, not against observed failure.

Why existing approaches struggle

Taxonomies force one label per event. Real failures have several causes, and labels drift over 10 years and across teams.
Cross-references are human-made, incomplete and inconsistent, so “incident X was caused by control gap Y” is mostly unrecorded.
Closure is measured as activity completion, not outcome.
Each object type is analysed in its own silo, so patterns spanning process, application, product and cause are invisible.

Why AI changes the outcome: Embeddings and reasoning models can read the narrative text across all four object types and group events by mechanism rather than by label. They can also infer missing links and judge whether an action description plausibly addresses a stated root cause. That is semantic work at a volume no analyst team can do.

