Part 3: Ranking
Remediation effectiveness and recurrence (2), built on pattern discovery (1)
Control effectiveness validation (4)
Control coverage gaps (3)
Product and customer outcome failure (9)
Early-warning and severity migration (6/7)

Why #1 wins: It answers the question boards and regulators actually care about, it needs no perfect links (recurrence can be detected from incident text and dates alone), and it produces dollar figures from the write-off data. Everything else in the top five reuses its patterns.

Why the others lose

Control validation and coverage gaps depend on a reliable link between patterns and controls, which is the weakest data. They come second because they consume #1’s output.
Product and customer outcome is high-value but depends on product extraction quality.
Early-warning is the most exciting and hardest to validate. Rare events give you little to test against.
Digital twin is premature without causal evidence.

Challenging my own ranking: Recurrence could be driven by reporting behaviour. A team that gets better at logging incidents will appear to have more recurrence. A “fix” might simply move events into a different category. And a pattern that vanishes after remediation may have vanished because the business changed. So #1 is only credible if recurrence is measured against exposure (volume, change activity) and with a reporting-culture adjustment. Without that, it is a persuasive wrong answer.

Part 4: Ten “we did not know that” insights
Fixes that never fixed: X% of closed high-rated issues have a near-identical incident pattern recurring within 24 months, concentrated in a handful of action types.
The most common remediation is the weakest: “Training and awareness” and “procedure update” actions show the lowest recurrence reduction, yet make up a large share of actions.
Phantom controls: Controls rated Effective that sit directly on top of a recurring failure pattern for years.
Uncontrolled failure: Patterns that cause material loss but have no semantically relevant control anywhere in the inventory.
Orphaned controls: Controls whose described objective matches no failure the bank has ever recorded, which may be waste or may be untested protection.
Same failure, different name: One mechanism appearing under many root cause labels across business units, so each unit sees a small problem and the bank has a large one.
Low-rated precursors: Minor incidents of a given pattern that statistically precede severe ones, with a typical lead time.
Change as the real root cause: A large fraction of failures trace to a particular type of change event (system migration, process handover, vendor change) regardless of the recorded cause.
Remediation half-life: How long a fix actually holds, by type and by business unit, and which owners’ fixes hold best.
Customer harm hidden in operations: Incidents rated low on financial impact but whose text shows repeated customer detriment, concentrated in specific products.

These are hypotheses until tested. Some may not survive.

Part 5: Unit of analysis

The failure pattern: a recurring mechanism combining a cause, a point in the process, a type of asset or product, and the conditions in which it occurs.

Why:

Incidents are too granular and noisy. Controls and issues are the bank’s own constructs and inherit its blind spots.
A pattern is the only unit that connects all the objects: it has incidents (evidence), issues (recognition), actions (response), controls (expected defence), processes and applications (location), and obligations (consequence).
It makes recurrence, effectiveness and coverage measurable as properties of the pattern.
It is robust to bad labels and bad links, because it is built from text and treats the human relationships as evidence to weigh.

The cost is that patterns are constructed, not observed. They are a model, and the definition choices (granularity, similarity threshold) shape the answers. That has to be governed.

Part 6: The one capability

Name: Failure Pattern Intelligence (recurrence and remediation effectiveness engine)

Objective: Maintain a living, evidence-based catalogue of how the bank fails, and measure whether remediation and controls change it.

Users: CRO and executive risk committee, Non-Financial Risk, Line 2 control assurance, Internal Audit (for risk-based planning), business unit risk leads, and regulators on request.

Outputs

Pattern catalogue with incident counts, loss, severity trend and recurrence rate
Remediation effectiveness score per action type, owner and business unit
Pattern-to-control coverage view, with confidence levels
Ranked list of “repeat failure” patterns for executive attention
Reviewable evidence trail from every claim back to source records

Why it matters: It shifts the question from “did we complete the action?” to “did the failure stop?”, and it directs control and remediation spend at demonstrated losses.

Part 7: Minimum viable methodology
Define the question and the decision it supports. Recurrence and fix effectiveness, with named decision-makers. Without this, the work drifts into exploration.
Profile and clean the text and dates. Establish how much usable narrative each object has and where history is thin. Embeddings of poor text produce confident nonsense.
Build a validation set by hand. Subject-matter experts label a few hundred incident pairs as same or different mechanism. This is the ground truth for everything after, and it is the step most often skipped.
Form candidate patterns from incident and issue text. Group by mechanism meaning, not by taxonomy. Tune against the validation set.
Attach issues, actions and controls to patterns. Use the human cross-references as one signal and text similarity as another, and record the confidence of each link.
Measure recurrence against exposure. Normalise for volume, change activity and reporting behaviour, so a rise in logging is not read as a rise in failure.
Test remediation effect. Compare pattern frequency and severity before and after action closure, against comparable patterns with no action. This is the first point at which a causal claim is even arguable.
Have experts review the top findings. Each headline finding gets a sample check and a stated confidence. This is the credibility gate.
Deliver to one executive forum, then measure use. Learn whether findings changed a decision before scaling.
Part 8: The sceptical regulator

Blind spots

Incident data is what people chose to report. Under-reported failure types, and near misses in weak-reporting units, are invisible.
Ten years of data spans changed systems, products and taxonomies. Old patterns may no longer apply.
Failures that have never happened, including the severe tail, cannot be learned from history.

Risks

Pattern definitions are model outputs. A different threshold gives different “recurrence”, so results can be steered.
Remediation “effectiveness” is correlational. Regression to the mean, business change and reporting shifts can all mimic a fix.
LLM-inferred links can look authoritative while being wrong, and errors compound when patterns are built on inferred links.

Potential misuse

Used to rank people or teams, it will change reporting behaviour and corrupt the data it relies on.
Used to justify cutting controls with no recent failures, which is exactly the control that may be working.
Used as a defence (“the AI found no issue”) against supervisory challenge.

Where it could mislead: A clean, precise-looking dashboard that hides wide uncertainty. Every output should carry its confidence and sample size, and the bank should keep humans accountable for conclusions.

Part 9: As CRO

Choice: B, the Failure Intelligence Platform.

Against A (Control Intelligence): It optimises the framework without testing it. It inherits the assumption that controls are the right lens, and its data (control text, ratings) is the most self-referential.
Against C (Risk Prediction): Prediction needs stable, well-labelled outcomes and exposure data. We have neither yet. Failure Intelligence is the prerequisite and would produce the labelled data prediction needs.
Against D (Digital Twin): Simulation needs a validated causal model. Building it first is the classic way to spend millions on a model nobody trusts.
For B: It uses the largest, most objective dataset, tolerates the weak relationship data, produces dollar-denominated findings within months, and yields the foundation (patterns, recurrence, effect estimates) that A, C and D would later use. If it fails, it fails cheaply and visibly at step 3 or 7.

The main risk to my choice is that findings are uncomfortable and the organisation does not act on them. That is a governance question, so sponsorship has to sit with the CRO, not a data team.

Part 10: Blank sheet

An operational failure memory with a causal model of the bank.

Drop the governance vocabulary. Treat the bank as a system of work (processes, applications, products, change events, people, third parties) and every incident, issue and action as an observation of that system under stress. The capability is a continuously updated model that answers:

Where and how does this system actually break, and under what conditions?
Which interventions changed that behaviour, and for how long?
Where are we exposed to a failure we have not yet seen, because a similar structure failed elsewhere?

Controls become one type of intervention among many (a system change, a staffing change, a vendor exit), evaluated by the same evidence. Risk assessment becomes a set of testable predictions about failure, scored against what then happens. The long-term shift is from risk as opinion, documented periodically, to risk as an empirically tested model of the organisation.

I would not start here. It is the destination, and the Failure Pattern Intelligence capability in Part 6 is its first honest step, because it builds the pattern catalogue and effect estimates the larger model needs.
